{"id": "tpcds-006", "level": "expert", "nl_description": "Benchmark relational schema for OLAP workloads over a fictional general merchandise retailer with three distinct selling channels (brick-and-mortar, internet, and mailed catalog). All three channels share a set of conformed dimension tables in a star/snowflake hybrid. The date spine has 73,049 entries covering two centuries; it carries both Gregorian calendar attributes (year, month, day-of-month, day-of-week, quarter) and fiscal calendar attributes, plus holiday and weekend binary flags and same-day cross-references to last year and last quarter. The time spine is a full 86,400-entry table (one per second of the day) with hour, minute, second, AM/PM, named shift (Night/Evening/Day), sub-shift, and meal time. The item dimension uses a type-2 SCD pattern: effective start/end date on each record, surrogate integer key plus a stable character business key. Item current price is log-normally distributed; wholesale cost is lower. Ten item categories each containing multiple item classes. Customer dimension: 100K per SF1. Customers reference a household demographics dimension (7200 rows) and a customer demographics dimension (1.92M rows, full cartesian of gender, marital status, education, purchase estimate bands, credit rating). Customer address is a separate dimension. Store dimension (12 per SF) tracks employees and floor space as physical attributes. Each fact table references date, time, item, customer, demographic dimensions, store, and a promotion. Quantity per transaction is uniform integer in [1, 100]. Each fact table row computes 6+ monetary derived columns. Returns reference the originating ticket and can appear across channels.", "ground_truth_schema": {"tables": [{"name": "DATE_DIM", "pk": "d_date_sk", "columns": [{"name": "d_date_sk", "data_type": "INT"}, {"name": "d_date_id", "data_type": "VARCHAR"}, {"name": "d_date", "data_type": "DATE"}, {"name": "d_month_seq", "data_type": "INT"}, {"name": "d_wee